        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Escalate slowly.</strong> Hot soapy water, then a vinyl siding cleaner, then mineral spirits with real dwell time, then a citrus remover, then a melamine sponge. Most dried deck stain gives way at the mineral spirits stage if you let the solvent sit rather than scrubbing at it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Never reach for acetone, lacquer thinner, MEK or a deck stripper. Those dissolve PVC as readily as they dissolve stain, and the dull etched patch they leave is permanent — a far worse outcome than the splash. Test every product somewhere hidden, and work on cool siding in shade.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Expect the cleaned area to look brighter than the wall around it on older siding, and wash outward to blend it. And next time, spend ten minutes masking the bottom courses — it is the cheapest insurance in the entire staining job.
          </p>
        </div>


